Name the party that sent a wrong share, instead of stopping without a name.

Against MP-SPDZ 0.4.3 (6 Jul 2026). Applies to two files, both headers.

What is wrong today
-------------------
`MaliciousShamirMC` detects an inconsistent Shamir sharing and throws

    mac_fail("inconsistent Shamir secret sharing")

which names nobody, and measurement says the situation is worse than that
string suggests. Corrupting party 3's preprocessing and corrupting party 1's
produce the *identical* line -- the `3` in `Fatal error at fp2000-0:3` is the
instruction offset, not a party. And when the bad data belongs to a party the
others reach later, they die on a dropped connection first, so the attribution
an operator would naturally reach for -- whose link went down -- points at
whichever process exited rather than at the party whose data was wrong.

Why it is fixable
-----------------
Shamir shares of a degree-`t` secret are an `RS[n, t+1]` codeword. At `n = 7`
and `t = 2` that is `RS[7,3]`, minimum distance 5, so Berlekamp-Welch corrects
`floor(4/2) = 2` errors -- *exactly the corruption threshold this deployment
tolerates*. Every corruption the protocol is designed to survive is also one it
could name, and the engine was doing detection on data that supports location.

What this changes
-----------------
Two things, and the second is the one that costs.

`reconstruct()` runs Berlekamp-Welch when the existing consistency check fails,
and names the parties whose shares do not lie on the recovered polynomial. Past
the decoding capacity it says so rather than guessing: a decoder that named
somebody when three parties lied would be worse than one that gave up.

`exchange()` becomes a full broadcast. `ShamirMC::exchange` is a *partial*
broadcast that, with `threshold` set to `2t`, makes each party a sender to the
`2t+1` nearest and no further. That is exactly enough to reconstruct a
degree-`2t` sharing and exactly one share short of locating two liars in a
degree-`t` one. `finalize_raw()` correspondingly reads `n` shares rather than
`2t+1`.

Because `finalize_raw()` now checks every prefix through all `n` shares,
`init_open()` also constructs Lagrange reconstruction factors through `n`.
Leaving the stock `2t+1` upper bound here would index empty coefficient vectors
for shares six and seven and crash every party before the consistency check.

An earlier draft of this patch changed only the read side, on the reading that
`POpen_Begin` calls `P.send_all()` so every share was already on the wire. That
is the unbatched path. The batched one is `exchange`, and it is what runs.

What it costs, measured on host-a at n=7, T=2, 128-bit
------------------------------------------------------
Each party goes from four correspondents per opening to six.

    online phase       8.000  ->  12.000 elements per party per multiplication   1.50x
    single-phase total 48.235 ->  64.236                                          1.33x
    rounds             unchanged in both cases

Behaviour, one byte flipped in one party's triples, four parties tested:

    corrupt P0 -> "inconsistent Shamir secret sharing sent by player 0"
    corrupt P1 -> "... sent by player 1"
    corrupt P3 -> "... sent by player 3"
    corrupt P6 -> "... sent by player 6"
    corrupt {1,4} -> "... sent by player 1, player 4"
    corrupt {0,2,5} -> "more than 2 parties sent wrong shares, which is beyond
                        the decoding capacity of this sharing"

What it does not do
-------------------
This is identifiable abort, not robustness. Naming the party and recovering the
opened value does not repair the bad triple underneath it, so the computation is
still wrong and still stops. Robust *reconstruction* is not robust
*computation*, and the distance between them is what Goyal-Song-Zhu's segmenting
and re-sharing is for.

It also cannot see a party that substituted its *input*. `secret_input()` sums
one additive share per party, so a substituted input is a valid sharing of a
different value: nothing is inconsistent and there is no codeword to decode.

A native verification model of the same decode covers the capacity boundaries
in the QOMM audit tests.

